  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure to our east continues to be an influence through\ntoday and into Wednesday. Dry weather and very warm to hot\ntemperatures are in store today and Wednesday afternoon as a\nresult. The lowlands can expect high temperatures in the mid 80s\nto lower 90s, while the mountains will stay between the low 70s\nand mid 80s.\n\nPatchy valley fog is expected tonight, but light winds will \nkeep it from being too dense, mostly across the lowlands.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA cold front approaches from the west late Wednesday permitting\nthe entrance of showers and thunderstorms Wednesday evening \ninto Thursday. A few of these storms could initially be strong \nor even severe across our Ohio counties Wednesday evening. This \nwill be conditional, depending mostly on where the best forcing \nof the front will be. Damaging wind gusts and maybe some small \nhail will be the main severe weather hazards. The severe weather\npotential wanes quite a bit on Thursday as cloud cover and slow\nfront motion may limit instability.\n\nThe main focus with this system will be the potential for\ntraining showers/thunderstorms containing heavy rainfall which\nwill lead to concerns for localized to isolated flash flooding\nconcerns, especially on Thursday. Models are suggesting between\n1.00\" and 2.00\" of rainfall will fall across northern WV and\nOH between Wednesday night and Friday. The heaviest of this \nwill fall Thursday morning and afternoon.\n\nPrecipitation will linger Thursday night through Friday morning,\nmostly across the mountains as the stubborn front finally \ntrudges out of the area. Chances for precipitation will exist\nthrough the weekend into early next week behind this front as a\nwarm front lifts up from the south and parks nearby.",
